Decisions in code allow programs to branch and respond to inputs.

### Strict Equality vs Loose Equality
Always use strict equality (`===`) instead of loose equality (`==`) to prevent unexpected type coercion.

```javascript
const score = 85;
if (score >= 90) {
  console.log("Grade: A");
} else if (score >= 70) {
  console.log("Grade: B");
} else {
  console.log("Needs revision");
}
```

Functions are first-class citizens in JavaScript: they can be passed as arguments, assigned to variables, and returned from other functions.

```javascript
// Arrow function syntax
const calculateTotal = (price, taxRate = 0.08) => {
  return price + (price * taxRate);
};
console.log(calculateTotal(100)); // 108
```

Modern JavaScript emphasizes declarative array manipulation over traditional for loops.

```javascript
const scores = [65, 82, 90, 45, 95];
// Filter passing scores
const passing = scores.filter(s => s >= 70);
// Double scores
const curved = scores.map(s => s + 5);
```

The Document Object Model (DOM) connects JavaScript to web page elements.

```javascript
const button = document.querySelector('#enroll-btn');
button.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  button.textContent = 'Enrolled!';
  button.classList.add('bg-emerald-600');
});
```

Web applications constantly fetch data across networks. Async/await allows you to write asynchronous code that reads like synchronous steps.

```javascript
async function loadCourses() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('/api/courses');
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network error');
    const data = await response.json();
    console.log(data);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Fetch failed:', error.message);
  }
}
```

Encapsulate repetitive logic with functions. Import standard libraries such as `math`, `random`, and `datetime`.

```python
def calculate_roi(investment, returns):
    gain = returns - investment
    return round((gain / investment) * 100, 2)
```

Defensive programming catches errors before they crash running scripts.

```python
try:
    user_input = int(input("Enter number: "))
    result = 100 / user_input
except ValueError:
    print("Please enter a valid integer.")
except ZeroDivisionError:
    print("Cannot divide by zero.")
else:
    print(f"Result: {result}")
```

Write base styles for mobile devices first. Use `min-width` queries to enhance the layout as screen real estate grows.

```css
.card-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  gap: 1rem;
}

@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .card-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }
}

@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  .card-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }
}
```

Instead of writing dozens of media queries for font sizes, use CSS `clamp(min, preferred, max)`.

```css
h1 {
  font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
}
```

React lets you build user interfaces out of individual pieces called components.

```tsx
interface CourseBadgeProps {
  title: string;
  isFree: boolean;
}

export function CourseBadge({ title, isFree }: CourseBadgeProps) {
  return (
    <div className="flex items-center gap-2 p-2">
      <span>{title}</span>
      <span>{isFree ? "Free" : "Premium"}</span>
    </div>
  );
}
```

State holds data that changes over time. Always treat React state as immutable.

```tsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
// Increment correctly with updater function
setCount(prev => prev + 1);
```

useEffect lets you synchronize a component with external systems (fetching data, setting timers, or subscribing to events).

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  async function load() {
    const res = await fetch('/api/courses');
    const data = await res.json();
    setCourses(data.courses);
  }
  load();
}, []); // Empty dependency array runs once on mount
```

In controlled components, input form values are driven by React state.

```tsx
const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
<input
  value={email}
  onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)}
  placeholder="Enter email"
/>
```

Custom hooks let you extract and reuse stateful logic across multiple components (e.g. `useLocalStorage`, `useDebounce`).

```tsx
function useDebounce<T>(value: T, delay: number): T {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [value, delay]);
  return debounced;
}
```

TypeScript adds static typing to JavaScript, catching typos, undefined property accesses, and incorrect argument types before code ever runs.

```typescript
let studentName: string = "Elena";
let completedLessons: number = 12;
let isGraduated: boolean = false;
```

Define explicit shapes for objects and functions.

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
  readonly id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  bio?: string; // Optional property
}
```

A variable can hold one of several types. Use `typeof`, `instanceof`, or literal tags to narrow types.

```typescript
type RequestState =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: string[] }
  | { status: 'error'; message: string };
```

Generics act like variables for types, allowing functions and data structures to work across any type while retaining full safety.

```typescript
function getFirstElement<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {
  return items[0];
}
const num = getFirstElement([10, 20, 30]); // Type is number
```

Type React elements with strict precision:

```typescript
interface ButtonProps {
  onClick: (e: React.MouseEvent<HTMLButtonElement>) => void;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}
```

Never trust client-supplied data. Validate types, string lengths, and schema invariants on the server before database execution.

```javascript
if (!body.email || !body.email.includes('@')) {
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Valid email required' }), { status: 400 });
}
```

Many users navigate exclusively with Tab, Enter, Space, and Arrow keys. Never use `outline: none` without supplying a high-contrast `:focus-visible` alternative!

```css
button:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid #2563eb;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```
